  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 201 PM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nWe are basically in a holding pattern until our \"main event\" arrives \nlater this evening and tonight. Current satellite features a mix of \npatchy upper-level cirrus draped and banded diurnal cu across \neastern MN and western WI while widespread stratus moves in \nfrom the Dakotas. Temperatures range in the mid-80s and are \nexpected to top out near 90 degrees this afternoon. Parts of \nsouthern and southwestern MN will be slightly cooler as cloud \ncover in those regions is a bit more prevalent. Haze on the \nhorizon remains in place as elevated wildfire smoke continues to\nlinger over the northern plains. It is expected though that the\ncontent of smoke decreases as we head into the weekend. \n\nWe continue to monitor the forward progress of a stacked low \npressure system which will move eastward tonight into Friday. This \nsystem continues to be advertised as a sufficient rain maker thanks \nto a deep moisture profile currently in place. It is expected \nas we progress into this evening, the pressure gradient from the\nlow will tighten and generate widespread heavy rainfall. At \nleast during the late afternoon/early evening hours, severe \nthunderstorms are possible however, but higher confidence for \nany severe storms to occur is mainly over SD/NE. There is a \npotential that a storm or two early this afternoon crosses our \nwestern MN border and produces a damaging wind gust or two \nbefore we transition to strictly a heavy rain threat. \n\nAs for forecasted amounts, confidence continues to hone in on a \nswath of QPF between 1-2\" with localized 4+\" of rain along and south \nof the MN River Valley and southwestern MN. Latest HREF 24-hr LPMM \ndoes try and cling onto rainfall amounts near 1\" as far north as I-\n94 however, given the continued trends amongst most HREF membership \nand other hi-res guidance, the more realistic amount for eastern MN \n(incl. the TC Metro) are closer to a few tenths of an inch at most. \nSimilar to snowfall events here, the gradient at which the highest \nrainfall amounts are found will be sharp. This system will be slow \nmoving and thus allow for areas to observe prolonged heavy rainfall, \ntherefore localized flooding/ponding in low-lying areas will be \npossible. At this time, it isn't until late Friday afternoon \nuntil precip clears. Aside from the rainfall, it will feel much \ncooler as temps will struggle to get out of the low to mid-70s.\n\nOnce this system passes, upper-level ridging will take hold over the \nnorthern plains. Skies will be mostly sunny with highs in the mid \n80s and lows in 50s to lower 60s just in time for the weekend. As we \nenter into early next week, another upper-level wave will track \nacross the Int'l border. This likely looks to produce another shot \nof rain/storms across northern MN but still some time left to \ndetermine the southern extent. Temperatures for the remainder of the \nperiod will range at or just below normal.",
